  "constraints": "* Input arrays/tensors are non-empty and well-formed\n* Floating point values are finite real numbers\n* Numerical operations must adhere to standard precision tolerances",
  "starterCode": "-- SQL ROW_NUMBER\nSELECT segment_id, username, score,\n       ROW_NUMBER() OVER(PARTITION BY segment_id ORDER BY score DESC, username ASC) AS rank\nFROM user_activity\nORDER BY segment_id, rank;",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **ROW_NUMBER** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\ndef sql_row_number(records: list) -> list:\n    parts = {}\n    for r in records: parts.setdefault(r['segment_id'], []).append(r)\n    out = []\n    for s, items in sorted(parts.items()):\n        for rank, it in enumerate(sorted(items, key=lambda x: (-float(x['score']), x['username'])), 1):\n            out.append({'segment_id': it['segment_id'], 'username': it['username'], 'score': float(it['score']), 'rank': rank})\n    return out\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
